.TH VOICE 3
.SH NAME
voice \- speech synthesis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a '#V' /dev
.PP
.B /dev/voice/ctl
.br
.B /dev/voice/phonemes
.br
.B /dev/voice/pcm
.br
.B /dev/voice/screen
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I voice
device turns phonemes into sound.
Write the sounds to
.BR phonemes ,
read the samples from
.BR pcm :
.IP
.EX
bind -a '#V' /dev
echo -n 'hɛlˈoʊ' > /dev/voice/phonemes
cp /dev/voice/pcm /tmp/hello.pcm
.EE
.PP
The samples are signed 16-bit, little-endian, one channel, at the rate
.B ctl
reports \(em 16000 Hz.
.B Pcm
holds the last utterance and is readable until the next write replaces it.
.PP
The symbols are espeak's
.SM IPA ,
which is what
.IR phon (2)
produces and what the neural voice behind
.IR say (1)
was trained on, so the same phonemes drive either.
A symbol this device does not know is skipped rather than refused: a screen
reader that stops dead on an unexpected diacritic is worse than one that
carries on without it.
.SS screen
What the interface has to say about itself, one announcement per line.
A read blocks until there is one.
.PP
It can also be written.
.IR Libtk (2)
is the usual writer but there is no reason it should be the only one: a program
that has finished a long job has as much right to say so as a button has to
name itself, and a person who cannot see the screen has no other way to be
told.
.IP
.EX
echo 'the backup has finished' > /dev/voice/screen
.EE
.PP
A written announcement counts as a person's business rather than the machine's
chatter, so it interrupts what is being said, which is most of what makes it
worth writing.
.PP
.IR Libtk (2)
writes here when the keyboard focus moves, which is the one place in the system
that knows a person's attention has changed, and \(em when
.B announce
is
.B all
\(em when text appears in a text widget, which is where a shell window's output
goes.
A button announces its own label \(em
.B "Save changes button"
\(em falling back on the widget's name where it has no text.
.PP
A control that has a state says it: a checkbutton is
.B "Wrap lines check button not checked"
and a radiobutton
.B "Every third radio button selected" .
Naming a control without its state answers the wrong question, and there
is no way to find out by listening otherwise.
A scale is the case with no label at all, so its value is the only thing
there is to say:
.B "scale 40" .
A list is read as the line its cursor is on \(em
.B "list Inbox"
\(em since which line you are on is the whole content of a listbox.
.PP
An entry is read too \(em
.B "entry notes.txt"
\(em unless it is masked with
.BR -show ,
which is what marks a secret and is set on exactly the fields that hold
one.
A field that said only
.B entry
could not be read by ear at all, which is a worse failure than the one
that refusing them all was avoiding; a masked field is still silent, and
a field that is not masked is already on the screen in clear.
A
.B text
widget is still never read: it can hold a whole shell session.
.PP
When the person changes one, what it became is announced on its own \(em
just
.B checked
, or the slider's new value \(em without repeating the label, since they
were told which control they were on when they arrived at it and a reader
that reads the whole thing back on every keypress is tiring to use.
Scales and lists both take the arrow keys, so this is not a mouse-only
path: it is the only way a person using their ears knows the key did
anything.
.PP
That makes the screen reader a pipeline of things that already exist:
.IP
.EX
speak /dev/voice/screen &
.EE
.PP
and nothing in it needs to know what a widget is.
.PP
Output is announced a line at a time.
A shell window inserts the characters you type through the same path its output
takes, so announcing every insertion would read each letter back as it was
pressed and then read the line again when it echoed; the fragments are held
until a newline, which makes both into one utterance and keeps
.B libtk
from having to know any of this.
.PP
Widgets holding text the person typed, entry and text, are deliberately not
read out on arrival: announcing the contents of a field when it takes focus is
how a screen reader reads a password aloud.
.PP
Moving the focus discards any output still queued.
That is a person acting: they have stopped caring about whatever was scrolling
past, and making them sit through it before hearing where they now are is the
most tiring thing a screen reader does.
Announcements the person's own actions produced are kept.
.PP
When the ring is full the
.I oldest
announcement is dropped.
A screen reader is saying where you are now, and a backlog of where you used to
be is worse than useless because you have to sit through it.
.SS ctl
Reading gives the rate, the pitch, the speed, how many samples are waiting, how
many announcements are queued
.RB ( waiting ),
how many of those came from a person acting rather than the machine talking
.RB ( urgent ),
and the announce level.
.PP
.B Urgent
is what a reader polls between chunks of sound to decide whether to stop.
Output does not interrupt output: if it did, anything printing faster than it
can be read would never be read at all, every line cut off by the next.
.TP
.BI announce " off|focus|all"
What reaches
.BR screen .
.B Focus
is the default: where the keyboard is.
.B All
adds text as it appears, which is a different order of chattiness \(em focus
changes when a person does something, and output arrives whenever the machine
feels like it.
.B Off
says nothing at all.
.TP
.BI pitch " hz"
The fundamental, 40 to 400.
.TP
.BI speed " percent"
How fast to say it, 20 to 800, against the durations in the table.
Higher is faster.
.SH "WHY THIS AND NOT THE MODEL"
.IR Say (1)
speaks through
.IR ml (3)
and a sixty megabyte
.SM VITS
model.
It sounds far better than this and cannot be the basis of a screen reader, for
two reasons that are not going to change.
It uses eleven operators the software backend does not implement, two hundred
and fifty-eight convolutions among them, so it does not run on native Inferno
at all.
And it costs upwards of a second an utterance, where a system that reads out
what you type has to answer before the next key is pressed.
.PP
So this is a formant synthesizer: three resonators driven by a buzz or a hiss,
which is how DECtalk and eSpeak work and why they are still what people
actually read screens with.
It is a few kilobytes of state, it runs anywhere, it is far faster than real
time, and it stays intelligible at the rates people really use.
It does not sound natural and is not trying to.
.PP
The two are not rivals.
The model is for narration, where the words are chosen in advance and quality
is the whole point; this is for the screen, where the words arrive unbidden
and latency is.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devvoice.c
.br
.B /libtk/ebind.c
.SH "TK NEEDS VOICE"
.B Libtk
calls this device's
.B voiceannounce
directly, so a configuration with
.B tk
in it must also have
.BR voice .
Every one in this tree does, and the configuration that has a voice and no Tk,
.BR emu-g ,
never links
.B libtk
at all.
The hook lives in the device rather than in
.B libtk
for that reason: the device is present in strictly more configurations.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR phon (2),
.IR ml (3),
.IR audio (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Writing an unknown verb to
.B ctl
or a pitch or speed outside its range is an error.
Unknown phonemes are not.
.SH BUGS
It takes phonemes, not text.
Turning English into phonemes needs a dictionary,
.IR phon (2)
has one, and there is no reason to have a second copy in the kernel: Limbo is
as native as C on a machine that runs Dis in the kernel.
.PP
It does not play anything.
The samples have to be carried to
.IR audio (3)
by whoever wanted them, which also means telling that device the rate and
channel count first \(em it infers nothing.
.PP
Three formants, no fourth or fifth, no nasal zero, and stops are a burst
without a closure.
The phoneme table is one set of vowel targets from the literature rather than
anything measured, and stress and length marks in the input are ignored, so
everything is said flat and at one speed.
.PP
Nothing here has been listened to by the people who wrote it.
What is checked is that the output has energy where the table says it should:
.B /ɑ/
comes back with peaks at 700 and 1100 Hz against targets of 730 and 1090, and
.B /i/
with one at 2300 against 2290.
That establishes the resonators are doing what they are told and says nothing
about whether the result is intelligible.
